        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            A manual transfer switch is a sub-panel that sits beside your main electrical panel. A handful of your household circuits are rerouted through it, and each one gets a switch with two positions: line, which draws power from the utility as normal, and generator, which draws from a portable generator plugged into an inlet on the outside wall. The switch makes it impossible for a circuit to be connected to both at once. That last point is the entire reason the product exists.
          </p>

          <p>
            We built this list from manufacturer specification sheets, the Reliance Pro/Tran 2 catalog, and retailer listings from Home Depot, Lowe's, Ace, Norwall, SupplyHouse and DuroMax. We did not install or bench-test these switches. We compared them on the things that decide whether a switch suits your house: amperage, the number and type of circuits, whether an inlet and cord are included, where the enclosure can be mounted, and whether watt meters are fitted.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also kept an eye on how easy each switch is to buy correctly. Several model numbers in this category differ by one character and describe quite different products, such as Reliance's 306A and 306A1, and some listings repeat specifications that belong to a sibling model. Where sources disagreed, we say so in the product notes rather than picking the more flattering figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Price was a secondary factor. Every option here costs far less than the electrician's labor to install it, so a switch that saves the electrician an hour of wiring often costs less in total than a cheaper box that needs more work. If you have not yet chosen how to connect a generator at all, our explainer on <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch types, cost and installation</a> covers the full picture first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Switch, Interlock or Meter Collar
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are three common ways to connect a portable generator to house wiring safely, and they suit different homeowners. A manual transfer switch moves a fixed set of circuits, usually six to ten, onto the generator. You decide which circuits those are when it is installed, and during an outage you flip only those switches. It is the most foolproof option for someone who does not want to think about the panel in the dark.
          </p>

          <p>
            An interlock kit is a metal plate fitted to your existing panel that physically prevents the main breaker and a generator breaker from being on at the same time. With the main off and the generator breaker on, the generator feeds the whole panel, and you choose which circuits to run by turning breakers on and off. It costs less in hardware and lets you power any circuit, but you have to manage the load yourself. Our comparison of an <a href="/interlock-kit-vs-transfer-switch/">interlock kit vs transfer switch</a> goes through that choice in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            A meter-collar transfer device, of which GenerLink is the best-known brand, fits between the electric meter and its socket and switches the house over when a generator is plugged in. Because it touches the utility meter, it is normally installed by or with the approval of the utility, and many utilities do not allow them at all. If your utility offers one, it can be the neatest installation of all; if it does not, it is not an option, which is why we have not ranked one here.
          </p>

          <p>
            Automatic transfer switches belong to standby generators rather than portables. They start the generator and switch the house over without anyone touching them, and they cost considerably more. If you are weighing a standby unit instead, see our guide to the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators for power outages</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Our 6 Picks
