        <div id="circuits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Choosing Your Circuits
          </h2>

          <p>
            A manual switch powers only the circuits wired into it, so choosing them is the most important decision in the whole job. Start with the loads that protect the house and keep it livable: the refrigerator, any freezer, the furnace blower and its controls, the sump pump if you have one, a well pump if you rely on one, and enough lighting and outlet circuits to charge phones and run a few lamps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Count those circuits before you choose between a six-circuit and a ten-circuit switch. A house with a well pump, a sump pump, a gas furnace and a kitchen refrigerator on its own circuit uses four positions before any lighting at all. A 240-volt well pump takes a double-pole position, which counts as two. Six circuits is usually enough for a small house on city water; ten suits most others.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some loads do not belong on a portable-generator switch. Central air conditioners, electric ranges, electric water heaters and electric dryers each draw more than a 30-amp generator can comfortably supply alongside anything else. If you need one of those, you need a larger generator and probably a 50-amp switch, or a standby system. Our guide to whether a <a href="/can-a-portable-generator-power-an-electric-water-heater/">portable generator can power an electric water heater</a> works through one of those cases.
          </p>

          <p>
            Label every circuit clearly at the switch, in words you can read by flashlight. In an outage, the person flipping switches may not be the person who planned the installation, and a label that says Fridge and Kitchen Outlets is worth far more than a circuit number.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inlet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Inlets, Cords and Plugs
          </h2>

          <p>
            The generator never plugs into the switch directly. It plugs into a weatherproof power inlet on an outside wall, and the inlet is wired back to the switch. The inlet's shape must match the generator's outlet: an L14-30 inlet for a 30-amp generator, a 50-amp inlet for a 50-amp generator. Kits like the Generac 6853 and Connecticut Electric G2 include a matching inlet and cord; switches like the Reliance 510C do not, so add them to the budget.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use a proper four-wire generator cord rated for the amperage, with the correct locking plug on each end. A 30-amp L14-30 cord needs heavy conductors, and longer cords need heavier ones still to avoid voltage drop. Our guide to <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/">generator extension cord size</a> covers the gauge for each length.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inlet placement matters more than people expect. The generator must run outdoors, well away from doors, windows and vents, so the inlet should be on a wall where the generator can sit at a safe distance with the exhaust pointing away from the house. Do not mount the inlet beside a bedroom window just because the panel is on the other side of that wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            An RV-style TT-30 outlet is not a substitute. It supplies 120 volts on a single leg, so it cannot feed a 120/240-volt switch properly and cannot run any 240-volt circuit. If your generator's only large outlet is a TT-30, it is better suited to a recreational vehicle than to a house switch; see our guide to the <a href="/best-rv-generator/">best RV generators</a> for that use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Permits, Electricians and Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every switch on this list connects to your home's main panel, and in most jurisdictions that work requires a permit and an inspection. Hire a licensed electrician. Manufacturers including Generac recommend professional installation, and an inspected installation protects you if anything goes wrong later, including with your homeowner's insurance.
          </p>

          <p>
            The total cost is the switch or kit plus the electrician's time, an inlet and cord if the kit does not include them, and the permit fee. Prewired kits shorten the job because the circuit whips arrive attached; the electrician still has to move each chosen circuit from the main panel into the switch, which is where most of the labor goes. The more circuits you choose, the longer it takes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Get two or three quotes, and give each electrician the same information: the switch you want, the generator model and outlet type, the circuits you want covered, and where you want the inlet. Ask whether the quote includes the permit and the inlet box. Quotes that differ widely often differ in what they include rather than in the rate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some people consider installing a switch themselves. Unless you are a licensed electrician, do not. Working inside a live panel can kill you, mistakes can create a backfeed hazard for utility workers, and an uninspected installation can complicate an insurance claim. The switch is the cheap part of this job; the expertise is what you are paying for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Backfeed and What the Code Requires
          </h2>

          <p>
            Backfeed is what happens when generator power flows out of your house wiring and back onto the utility lines. The transformer on the pole then steps that power up to thousands of volts, which can electrocute a line worker who believes the line is dead. It can also damage your generator when grid power returns. This is why plugging a generator into a dryer outlet with a double-ended cord, sometimes called a suicide cord, is dangerous and illegal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The National Electrical Code addresses this in Article 702, which covers optional standby systems such as a home generator. It requires transfer equipment that prevents the generator and the utility from being connected at the same time. A listed manual transfer switch, a listed interlock kit and a utility-approved meter collar all meet that intent. Utilities say the same thing in their own generator guidance: use approved transfer equipment, never a cord into an outlet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use listed equipment and install it as listed. Interlock kits in particular must match the panel's brand and model; a generic plate that does not fit your panel exactly may not hold the breakers apart reliably. Ask the electrician to confirm the listing for your panel before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other serious hazard is carbon monoxide. Run the generator outdoors only, never in a garage even with the door open, and keep it well away from doors, windows and vents. Fit battery-powered carbon monoxide alarms inside the house. Many newer generators include CO shutoff features, but they are a backstop, not a reason to run a generator close to the house.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="using" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Using a Switch in an Outage
          </h2>

          <p>
            The routine is the same for every manual switch. Set the generator up outdoors at a safe distance, connect the cord to the generator and to the inlet, and start the generator with nothing connected. Let it run for a minute to stabilize. Then move the switches over to generator one at a time, starting with the largest motor load, such as the well pump or refrigerator.
          </p>

          <p>
            Switching one circuit at a time keeps the generator from being hit by several motor starts at once, which is the most common way people stall a generator. If your switch has watt meters, watch them as you go and keep the two legs roughly balanced. If it does not, add loads slowly and listen for the engine laboring.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the grid comes back, reverse the process. Move every switch back to line, then shut the generator down and disconnect the cord. Leave the generator to cool before refueling it. Never refuel a running or hot generator.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test the whole chain once a year, ideally before storm season: start the generator, run it through the switch for half an hour with real loads, and check the cord and inlet for damage. A switch that has never been used is a switch you do not know works, and the middle of an outage is the wrong time to find out. Pair the test with routine generator maintenance, such as <a href="/how-often-to-change-generator-oil/">changing the generator oil</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Switch Before Checking the Generator Outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The switch, inlet and cord all follow the generator's largest 240-volt outlet. Buy a 30-amp L14-30 kit for a generator with a 50-amp outlet and you cap it at roughly 7,200 watts. Read the outlet face on the generator first, then choose the switch, inlet and cord to match it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Wiring the Wrong Circuits</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A manual switch powers only the circuits wired into it. Leave out the sump pump or the furnace and you will not get them during an outage. List the loads you need, count the positions they take, including double-pole positions for 240-volt loads, and choose a switch with room for all of them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using an RV Outlet to Feed a House Switch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A TT-30 RV outlet supplies 120 volts on one leg. It cannot feed a 120/240-volt switch properly and cannot run any 240-volt load. If your generator has only an RV outlet, use it for an RV, or choose a generator with an L14-30 outlet for home backup.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Permit and Electrician</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Transfer equipment connects to your main panel, and most jurisdictions require a permit and inspection. A wiring mistake can backfeed the grid or start a fire, and an uninspected installation can complicate an insurance claim. Hire a licensed electrician, and get the inspection.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the Generator Too Close to the House</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The inlet decides where the generator sits. Mounting it beside a window or door puts exhaust where it can get inside. Place the inlet on a wall where the generator can run well away from openings, with the exhaust pointing away, and keep carbon monoxide alarms working inside.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

