          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best manual transfer switch for a portable generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a generator with a 30-amp L14-30 outlet, the Generac HomeLink 6853 kit is our top pick because it arrives prewired with an inlet box and cord and can later be upgraded for a standby generator. For a generator with a 50-amp outlet, the Reliance Controls 510C is the better choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a 30 amp or 50 amp transfer switch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Match the switch to your generator's largest 120/240-volt outlet. An L14-30 outlet calls for a 30-amp switch, which handles up to about 7,200 watts at 240 volts. A 50-amp outlet calls for a 50-amp switch, which can use up to 12,000 watts. A larger switch on a smaller generator adds cost without adding capacity.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an interlock kit as safe as a transfer switch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A listed interlock kit installed on a compatible panel prevents backfeed just as a transfer switch does, by stopping the main breaker and the generator breaker from being on together. The difference is convenience: an interlock lets you power any circuit but requires you to manage the load at the panel yourself.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a manual transfer switch myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Unless you are a licensed electrician, no. The work happens inside your main panel, most jurisdictions require a permit and inspection, and mistakes can electrocute you or a utility worker. Manufacturers such as Generac recommend professional installation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many circuits do I need on a transfer switch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Count your essential loads: refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower, sump pump, well pump, and a few lighting and outlet circuits. Remember that 240-volt loads such as a well pump use two positions. Six circuits suits a small house on city water; ten suits most other homes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a transfer switch run my central air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on a typical portable generator and 30-amp switch. Central air draws too much, especially when it starts, to run alongside other loads on about 7,000 watts. Running central air generally needs a large generator, a 50-amp connection, often a soft-start device, or a standby system.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a meter collar transfer switch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a device that fits between your electric meter and its socket and switches the house to generator power when a generator is plugged in. GenerLink is the best-known brand. Because it involves the utility meter, it usually needs utility approval, and many utilities do not allow them.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
